{"tool_name":"virhostnet","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"virhostnet","home":"http://virhostnet.prabi.fr/","license":"Not licensed","summary":"Virus-Host Network is a knowledge-base system dedicated to the curation, the integration, the management and the analysis of virus-host molecular (mainly protein-protein) interaction networks as well as their functional annotation (molecular functions, cellular pathways, protein domains). VirHostNet contains high quality and up-to-date information gathered and curated from public databases.","addition_date":"2017-03-30T22:04:35Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:56:24.318048Z","tool_type":["Bioinformatics portal","Database portal","Web service"],"version":["2.0"],"collections":["COVID-19","PRABI"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:virhostnet","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"vincent.navratil@univ-lyon1.fr","name":"VirHostNet Support","note":"Head of the prabi-amsb bioinformatics core facility (http://amsb.prabi.fr)","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9974-1877","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://pbil.univ-lyon1.fr/redmine/projects/virhostscape/wiki"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["vincent.navratil@univ-lyon1.fr"],"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Network analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3927"},{"term":"Pathway or network comparison","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0277"},{"term":"Pathway or network prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3439"},{"term":"Pathway or network visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3083"},{"term":"Protein interaction analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2949"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"VirHostnet","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"vincent.navratil@univ-lyon1.fr","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bib/bbaa232","metadata":{"abstract":"SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) is a novel virus of the family Coronaviridae. The virus causes the infectious disease COVID-19. The biology of coronaviruses has been studied for many years. However, bioinformatics tools designed explicitly for SARS-CoV-2 have only recently been developed as a rapid reaction to the need for fast detection, understanding and treatment of COVID-19. To control the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic, it is of utmost importance to get insight into the evolution and pathogenesis of the virus. In this review, we cover bioinformatics workflows and tools for the routine detection of SARS-CoV-2 infection, the reliable analysis of sequencing data, the tracking of the COVID-19 pandemic and evaluation of containment measures, the study of coronavirus evolution, the discovery of potential drug targets and development of therapeutic strategies. For each tool, we briefly describe its use case and how it advances research specifically for SARS-CoV-2. All tools are free to use and available online, either through web applications or public code repositories. Contact:evbc@unj-jena.de","authors":[{"name":"Almeida A."},{"name":"Aouacheria A."},{"name":"Arighi C."},{"name":"Bateman A."},{"name":"Baumbach J."},{"name":"Beerenwinkel N."},{"name":"Brandt C."},{"name":"Cacciabue M."},{"name":"Chuguransky S."},{"name":"Drechsel O."},{"name":"Finn R.D."},{"name":"Fritz A."},{"name":"Fuchs S."},{"name":"Hattab G."},{"name":"Hauschild A.-C."},{"name":"Heider D."},{"name":"Hoffmann M."},{"name":"Holzer M."},{"name":"Hoops S."},{"name":"Hufsky F."},{"name":"Kaderali L."},{"name":"Kalvari I."},{"name":"Kmiecinski R."},{"name":"Kuhnert D."},{"name":"Lamkiewicz K."},{"name":"Lasso G."},{"name":"Libin P."},{"name":"List M."},{"name":"Lochel H.F."},{"name":"Martin M.J."},{"name":"Martin R."},{"name":"Marz M."},{"name":"Matschinske J."},{"name":"McHardy A.C."},{"name":"Mendes P."},{"name":"Mistry J."},{"name":"Navratil V."},{"name":"Nawrocki E.P."},{"name":"O'Toole A.N."},{"name":"Ontiveros-Palacios N."},{"name":"Petrov A.I."},{"name":"Rangel-Pineros G."},{"name":"Redaschi N."},{"name":"Reimering S."},{"name":"Reinert K."},{"name":"Reyes A."},{"name":"Richardson L."},{"name":"Robertson D.L."},{"name":"Sadegh S."},{"name":"Singer J.B."},{"name":"Theys K."},{"name":"Upton C."},{"name":"Von Kleist M."},{"name":"Welzel M."},{"name":"Williams L."}],"citationCount":105,"date":"2021-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Briefings in Bioinformatics","title":"Computational strategies to combat COVID-19: Useful tools to accelerate SARS-CoV-2 and coronavirus research"},"pmcid":"PMC7665365","pmid":"33147627"},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkn794","metadata":{"abstract":"Infectious diseases caused by viral agents kill millions of people every year. The improvement of prevention and treatment of viral infections and their associated diseases remains one of the main public health challenges. Towards this goal, deciphering virus-host molecular interactions opens new perspectives to understand the biology of infection and for the design of new antiviral strategies. Indeed, modelling of an infection network between viral and cellular proteins will provide a conceptual and analytic framework to efficiently formulate new biological hypothesis at the proteome scale and to rationalize drug discovery. Therefore, we present the first release of VirHostNet (Virus-Host Network), a public knowledge base specialized in the management and analysis of integrated virus-virus, virus-host and host-host interaction networks coupled to their functional annotations. VirHostNet integrates an extensive and original literature-curated dataset of virus-virus and virus-host interactions (2671 non-redundant interactions) representing more than 180 distinct viral species and one of the largest human interactome (10 672 proteins and 68 252 non-redundant interactions) reconstructed from publicly available data. The VirHostNet Web interface provides appropriate tools that allow efficient query and visualization of this infected cellular network. Public access to the VirHostNet knowledge-based system is available at http://pbildb1.univ-lyon1.fr/virhostnet. \u00a9 2008 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Andre P."},{"name":"De chassey B."},{"name":"Delmotte S."},{"name":"Gautier C."},{"name":"Lotteau V."},{"name":"Meyniel L."},{"name":"Navratil V."},{"name":"Rabourdin-Combe C."}],"citationCount":139,"date":"2009-01-09T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"VirHostNet: A knowledge base for the management and the analysis of proteome-wide virus-host interaction networks"},"pmcid":"PMC2686459","pmid":"18984613"},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gku1121","metadata":{"abstract":"VirHostNet release 2.0 (http://virhostnet.prabi.fr) is a knowledgebase dedicated to the network-based exploration of virus-host protein-protein interactions. Since the previous VirhostNet release (2009), a second run of manual curation was performed to annotate the new torrent of high-throughput protein-protein interactions data from the literature. This resource is shared publicly, in PSI-MI TAB 2.5 format, using a PSICQUIC web service. The new interface of VirHostNet 2.0 is based on Cytoscape web library and provides a user-friendly access to the most complete and accurate resource of virus-virus and virus-host protein-protein interactions as well as their projection onto their corresponding host cell protein interaction networks. We hope that the VirHostNet 2.0 system will facilitate systems biology and gene-centered analysis of infectious diseases and will help to identify new molecular targets for antiviral drugs design. This resource will also continue to help worldwide scientists to improve our knowledge on molecular mechanisms involved in the antiviral response mediated by the cell and in the viral strategies selected by viruses to hijack the host immune system.","authors":[{"name":"Delmotte S."},{"name":"Guirimand T."},{"name":"Navratil V."}],"citationCount":176,"date":"2015-01-28T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"VirHostNet 2.0: Surfing on the web of virus/host molecular interactions data"},"pmcid":"PMC4383936","pmid":"25392406"}],"topic":[{"term":"Infectious disease","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3324"},{"term":"Molecular interactions, pathways and networks","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0602"},{"term":"Protein interaction experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3957"},{"term":"Protein interactions","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0128"},{"term":"Virology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0781"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"VirHostnet","home":"https://bio.tools/virhostnet","license":"Not licensed","version":"2.0","summary":"Virus-Host Network is a knowledge-base system dedicated to the curation, the integration, the management and the analysis of virus-host molecular (mainly protein-protein) interaction networks as well as their functional annotation (molecular functions, cellular pathways, protein domains). VirHostNet contains high quality and up-to-date information gathered and curated from public databases.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:virhostnet:2.0/cmd/virhostnet.prabi.fr","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"license":{"open_source":false,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":82,"citations":[{"count":3,"year":"2020"},{"count":3,"year":"2026"},{"count":4,"year":"2025"},{"count":7,"year":"2024"},{"count":12,"year":"2023"},{"count":26,"year":"2021"},{"count":27,"year":"2022"}],"doi":"10.1093/bib/bbaa232","pmcid":"PMC7665365","pmid":"33147627","ref_count":113,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1999"},{"count":1,"year":"2002"},{"count":1,"year":"2003"},{"count":1,"